{"chatbot":{"body":{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**Name:** D augmented (D+).  \r\n**Construction:** Root = D, minor third = F, raised fifth = A♯ (an augmented fifth).  \r\n\r\n**Typical harmonic function:**  \r\n- **As a dominant alteration:** In many keys D+ serves as a **V⁺** chord, the dominant of the tonic with the fifth raised to create a stronger pull toward the resolution (e.g., V⁺ → I in G minor or G major).  \r\n- **As a secondary dominant:** It can also function as **V⁺/V** (the leading‑tone chord of the dominant) when the music is heading to the V chord of the key.  \r\n\r\nThe exact role depends on the surrounding chords, but the hallmark of D+ is its “augmented‑dominant” tension that wants to resolve up a half‑step (usually to the tonic or to the dominant).","routing":"low-confidence-fallback"},"error":null,"ms":4667,"status":200,"url":"http://127.0.0.1:5252/api/chatbot/chat"},"expect":{"chords":["D","F","A#"],"kind":"identify"},"ga":["keys/D minor","keys/Bb major","keys/D major"],"ga_res":[{"body":[{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"Dm","function":"Tonic","functionalDescription":"Aeolian degree 1 (Aeolian Degree1 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["D","F","A"],"romanNumeral":"i","root":"D","scaleDegree":1,"templateName":"Aeolian Degree1 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"Edim","function":"Supertonic","functionalDescription":"Aeolian degree 2 (Aeolian Degree2 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["E","G","A♯"],"romanNumeral":"ii°","root":"E","scaleDegree":2,"templateName":"Aeolian Degree2 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"F","function":"Mediant","functionalDescription":"Aeolian degree 3 (Aeolian Degree3 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["F","A","C"],"romanNumeral":"III","root":"F","scaleDegree":3,"templateName":"Aeolian Degree3 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"Gm","function":"Subdominant","functionalDescription":"