{"chatbot":{"body":{"agent":"theory","answer":"D harmonic minor is the minor scale on D that raises the seventh degree to a leading tone (C♯). In pitch‑class notation the scale consists of the notes D‑E‑F‑G‑A‑B♭‑C♯, which correspond to the set class 7‑34 (ordered as {0,1,3,4,5,7,8} and transposed to D becomes {2,4,5,7,9,10,1}).\r\n\r\n**Scale‑degree formula**: 1‑2‑♭3‑4‑5‑♭6‑7 (the 7 is a major, i.e., a leading tone, distinguishing it from the natural‑minor seventh).\r\n\r\n**Di­atonic triads in D harmonic minor**: \r\n- i  – D minor (D‑F‑A) \r\n- ii° – E diminished (E‑G‑B♭) \r\n- III+ – F augmented (F‑A‑C♯) \r\n- iv – G minor (G‑B♭‑D) \r\n- V  – A major (A‑C♯‑E) or A7 (A‑C♯‑E‑G) for a strong dominant function \r\n- VI – B♭ major (B♭‑D‑F) \r\n- vii° – C♯ diminished (C♯‑E‑G).\r\n\r\nThese chords are especially useful for guitarists: the dominant‑V seventh (A7) creates the characteristic tension that resolves to the tonic D minor because the leading tone C♯ resolves up a half‑step to D.\r\n\r\n**Voice‑leading notes**: \r\n- The leading tone C♯ tends to resolve stepwise up to the tonic D. \r\n- The lowered sixth B♭ often moves stepwise to A (down a whole step) or to C (up a whole step) in melodic lines, providing smooth voice leading.\r\n\r\n**Relation to other minor forms**:\r\n- *Natural minor*: D‑E‑F‑G‑A‑B♭‑C. No raised seventh; the seventh (C) does not function as a leading tone.\r\n- *Melodic minor (ascending)*: raises both the sixth and seventh, giving D‑E‑F‑G‑A‑B‑C♯. Descending, it usually reverts to the natural minor (B♭ and C). This is a common practice on guitar, especially in melodic passages.\r\n\r\n**Practical tip**: Many guitar pieces borrow the raised seventh (C♯) for the V7 chord while retaining the natural sixth (B♭) elsewhere, which is a hallmark of harmonic‑minor usage.\r\n\r\nIf you can share a specific chord progression or melodic excerpt, I can pinpoint the mode and suggest appropriate voicings.","routing":"llm"},"error":null,"ms":16960,"status":200,"url":"http://127.0.0.1:5252/api/chatbot/chat"},"ga":["keys/D minor","keys/F major"],"ga_res":[{"body":[{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"Dm","function":"Tonic","functionalDescription":"Aeolian degree 1 (Aeolian Degree1 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["D","F","A"],"romanNumeral":"i","root":"D","scaleDegree":1,"templateName":"Aeolian Degree1 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"Edim","function":"Supertonic","functionalDescription":"Aeolian degree 2 (Aeolian Degree2 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["E","G","A♯"],"romanNumeral":"ii°","root":"E","scaleDegree":2,"templateName":"Aeolian Degree2 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"F","function":"Mediant","functionalDescription":"Aeolian degree 3 (Aeolian Degree3 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["F","A","C"],"romanNumeral":"III","root":"F","scaleDegree":3,"templateName":"Aeolian Degree3 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"Gm","function":"Subdominant","functionalDescription":"Aeolian degree 4 (Aeolian Degree4 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["G","A♯","D"],"romanNumeral":"iv","root":"G","scaleDegree":4,"templateName":"Aeolian Degree4 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"Am","function":"Dominant","functionalDescription":"Aeolian degree 5 (Aeolian Degree5 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["A","C","E"],"romanNumeral":"v","root":"A","scaleDegree":5,"templateName":"Aeolian Degree5 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"A♯","function":"Submediant","functionalDescription":"Aeolian degree 6 (Aeolian Degree6 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["A♯","D","F"],"romanNumeral":"VI","root":"A♯","scaleDegree":6,"templateName":"Aeolian Degree6 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"C","function":"LeadingTone","functionalDescription":"Aeolian degree 7 (Aeolian Degree7 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":tru