{"chatbot":{"body":{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"**Transposed chords**\r\n\r\n| Original key | Original chords | New key | Transposed chords |\r\n|-------------|----------------|---------|-------------------|\r\n| E           | E – A – B      | D       | **D – G – A**     |\r\n\r\nSo replace every **E** with **D**, every **A** with **G**, and every **B** with **A**.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Open‑position shapes\r\n\r\n| Chord | Frets (low E → high e) | Diagram (VexTab) |\r\n|-------|------------------------|------------------|\r\n| D     | x‑x‑0‑2‑3‑2            | ```vextab<br>tabstave<br>notes :w (0/4.2/3.3/2/2/1)``` |\r\n| G     | 3‑2‑0‑0‑0‑3            | ```vextab<br>tabstave<br>notes :w (3/6.2/5.0/4.0/3.0/1)``` |\r\n| A     | x‑0‑2‑2‑2‑0            | ```vextab<br>tabstave<br>notes :w (0/5.0/4.2/3.2/2.2/1)``` |\r\n\r\n*(x = muted, 0 = open)  \r\n\r\nThese shapes sit comfortably under the vocal range of most singers who find the original key too high.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Simple strumming pattern (4/4)\r\n\r\n```\r\n↓   ↑   ↓ ↑   ↓   ↑   ↓ ↑\r\n1   &   2 &   3   &   4 &\r\n```\r\n\r\nApply it to each chord in the progression D – G – A (or repeat D‑G‑A‑D for a full‑cycle verse).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### Quick capo tip\r\n\r\nIf you prefer the feel of the original E‑shape but need the pitch of D, place a **Capo on 2nd fret** and play the original E‑A‑B shapes. The capo will raise everything by a whole step, giving you the same sounding chords (D‑G‑A) while you keep familiar fingerings.  \r\n\r\nUse whichever option feels most comfortable under the fingers. Happy playing!","routing":"low-confidence-fallback"},"error":null,"ms":1669,"status":200,"url":"http://127.0.0.1:5252/api/chatbot/chat"},"expect":{"chords":["E","A","B"],"dst":1,"kind":"transpose","src":0},"ga":["keys/E major","keys/D major"],"ga_res":[{"body":[{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"E","function":"Tonic","functionalDescription":"Ionian degree 1 (Ionian Degree1 Triad)","isNaturall