{"chatbot":{"body":{"agent":"fallback-direct","answer":"Yes. The note **E** is common to both an E‑major (or E‑minor) chord and a C‑major chord.\r\n\r\n| Chord | Notes | Common tone with the other |\r\n|-------|-------|----------------------------|\r\n| **E maj** | E – G♯ – B | **E** (root) |\r\n| **C maj** | C – E – G | **E** (3rd) |\r\n\r\nSo when you play the two chords together, the E string can be left ringing as the shared pitch.\r\n\r\n**Open‑string voicings showing the common E**\r\n\r\nE major (open shape)  \r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (0/6.0/5.2/4.2/3.1/2.0/1)\r\n```\r\n\r\nC major (open shape)  \r\n\r\n```vextab\r\ntabstave\r\nnotes :w (3/5.x/4.2/3.0/2.1/1.0)\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn the E‑major shape the low E string (6th string open) is the common tone, and in the C‑major shape the 1st‑string high‑E (open) is the same pitch. You can let either of those open E strings ring through a progression from C to E to keep the shared note sustained.","routing":"low-confidence-fallback"},"error":null,"ms":1871,"status":200,"url":"http://127.0.0.1:5252/api/chatbot/chat"},"expect":{"chords":["C","E"],"kind":"common"},"ga":["keys/C major","keys/E major"],"ga_res":[{"body":[{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"C","function":"Tonic","functionalDescription":"Ionian degree 1 (Ionian Degree1 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["C","E","G"],"romanNumeral":"I","root":"C","scaleDegree":1,"templateName":"Ionian Degree1 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"Dm","function":"Supertonic","functionalDescription":"Ionian degree 2 (Ionian Degree2 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["D","F","A"],"romanNumeral":"ii","root":"D","scaleDegree":2,"templateName":"Ionian Degree2 Triad"},{"alternateNames":[],"commonality":1,"contextualName":"Em","function":"Mediant","functionalDescription":"Ionian degree 3 (Ionian Degree3 Triad)","isNaturallyOccurring":true,"notes":["E","G","B"],"romanNumeral":"iii","root":"E","scaleDegree":3,